"""Two-tier typing stubs (PLAN §8), generated by ``scripts/gen_stubs.py``.

The *lazy* tier is what the surface exposes: every op returns another lazy
type, and ``collect()`` -- defined once on the generic ``Lazy[T]`` base --
returns the *materialised* tier (the engine models ``Document`` / ``Reference``
/ ``Field`` / ``Collection``). At runtime every lazy value is one generic
``Expr``; these Protocols exist only for the type checker (``lazy(cls)`` / the
client facade cast an ``Expr`` to the matching lazy tier). Keeping the tier a
stub -- not a real class -- preserves the independent, generic recorder.

``Lazy[T]`` is the bridge the client uses for ``execute``: a lazy input of any
tier maps to its materialised ``T`` (``wc.execute(wc.fetch(u)) -> Document``).

The class bodies below the marker are GENERATED from the model ops so they
cannot drift -- edit the ops (or the generator), never this block:

    python scripts/gen_stubs.py          # rewrite
    python scripts/gen_stubs.py --check  # CI / test_typing
"""
